                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238581159",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198028229040",
                            "num_reviews": 59,
                            "playtime_forever": 2138,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 1,
                            "playtime_at_review": 2138,
                            "last_played": 1792999341
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Metal Gear Solid V: The Phantom Pain is one of the most impressive stealth games I’ve ever played, but also one of the most difficult and demanding experiences I’ve had in this genre. this is definitely not a game where you can simply run into an enemy base, shoot everyone and expect everything to work out, because even a small mistake can turn a perfectly planned infiltration into complete chaos. honestly, there were plenty of moments when I felt completely lost, frustrated or overwhelmed by the enemies, but at the same time I had an incredible amount of fun with it. the more I played, the more I started understanding its mechanics and discovering new ways to approach missions, and that feeling of gradually getting better is one of the things I enjoyed the most. the game can be brutally unforgiving when you make mistakes, but it also gives you an incredible amount of freedom to figure things out for yourself, and that makes every successful mission feel genuinely rewarding.\r\n\r\nhttps://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3816733556\r\n\r\nthe gameplay is easily the biggest highlight. unlike many stealth games where you follow a fairly predictable routine, The Phantom Pain gives you enormous freedom to decide how you want to infiltrate an enemy base. you can scout the area with binoculars, mark enemies, study their patrol routes, sneak through the darkness, disable communications, distract guards or simply wait for the right opportunity to move forward. you can also use weapons, explosives, vehicles and all kinds of equipment when things go wrong, although relying on direct combat can quickly become a terrible idea when reinforcements start arriving. what I particularly love is how the game makes you feel responsible for your own mistakes. if a guard spots you because you rushed into an open area without checking your surroundings, you usually have nobody to blame but yourself. at the same time, when you manage to sneak into a heavily guarded location, complete your objective and escape without anyone realizing you were there, it feels fantastic. there is something incredibly satisfying about completing a difficult mission through patience and preparation instead of simply overpowering every enemy in your way.\r\n\r\nanother major highlight is the open world and the amount of tools available to you. the environments are enormous, and even though they can sometimes feel a little empty when you are travelling between objectives, the actual missions offer plenty of opportunities to experiment. the Fulton recovery system is one of my favorite mechanics because you can extract enemy soldiers and bring them to Mother Base, gradually building your own private army and expanding your available resources. you can recruit soldiers with different abilities, develop new weapons and gadgets, improve your equipment and unlock new possibilities that make later missions feel different from your first attempts. your support options also become increasingly useful, whether you need supplies delivered during a mission, a buddy to help you infiltrate an enemy position or backup when everything goes wrong. the game constantly gives you new toys to experiment with, and the more you invest in Mother Base, the more possibilities become available. it creates an addictive gameplay loop where completing missions is not just about reaching the next objective, but also about improving your resources and preparing yourself for whatever comes next.\r\n\r\nhttps://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3816733287\r\n\r\nthe graphics and presentation are also impressive, especially considering when the game was originally released. the environments look great, the lighting is excellent and the animations make Snake feel believable when crawling through the grass, climbing obstacles or carefully moving around enemy positions. the sound design is equally important because listening to enemy conversations, footsteps and nearby activity can make the difference between a successful infiltration and a complete disaster. the story, however, is a little more complicated. there are some genuinely interesting moments and characters, but the narrative can feel fragmented, with long stretches of gameplay between important story developments. the mission structure also becomes repetitive at times, especially when you start revisiting locations or completing objectives that feel similar to things you have already done. the second half of the campaign is particularly uneven, and I wish the story had received a more satisfying conclusion. despite that, the freedom of the gameplay kept me interested even when the narrative was not moving forward as much as I wanted.\r\n\r\nhttps://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3794363051\r\n\r\noverall, Metal Gear Solid V: The Phantom Pain is an incredible game that can be extremely difficult, sometimes frustrating and occasionally repetitive, but I had a fantastic time playing it. mastering its mechanics takes patience, and there were definitely moments when I had to stop, rethink my approach and try again, but that is also what makes the experience so rewarding. this is a game that respects your ability to learn, adapt and experiment instead of constantly holding your hand, and once everything starts clicking, infiltrating enemy bases becomes an experience that very few other games can match. I also have a personal reason to remember this adventure fondly, since this game was a birthday present from my dear friend Thaii. that makes finishing it feel even more special to me, because now I have another great gaming experience connected to a gift from someone who means a lot to me. so, despite its flaws and its demanding difficulty, I can easily recommend The Phantom Pain to anyone who enjoys stealth, tactical gameplay and the freedom to approach situations in their own way. it may test your patience more than once, but the satisfaction of overcoming its challenges makes the journey absolutely worth it.\r\n\r\n🌸🌷🌹🌺🪻💐🌼🌻🌷🌸🌹\r\n\r\nmy sweet little thaiiiii, my precious frienddddd!! 💗🥹🌷🌸 i wish i could send you an entire garden full of flowers, because you deserve all the most beautiful and delicate affection in the world!! 🌹🪻🌺🌷🌼 every little flower here represents a tiny piece of the happiness you bring me, the friendship i treasure so dearly, and all the special memories you leave in my heart, including this wonderful little game that became another unforgettable adventure for your batsy!! 🎮💗🌸\r\n\r\nthank you for being such a lovely, sweet and special person, thaii!! 🥹💕 may life fill your days with flowers, sweet treats, fluffy little clouds, warm hugs and a million reasons to smile!! 🌷🍓🌸🧁🌺☁️💐 you deserve an entire garden blooming just to brighten your path, with every single petal carrying a little affectionate kiss from your batsy!! 🦇💖🌹\r\n\r\nsending you millions of long, sweet kisses, the warmest and tightest hugs, and the biggest bouquet of affection everrrrr!! 😚😚😚💗💗💗\r\n\r\n🌸🌷🌹🌺🪻💐🌼🌻🌸🌷🌹🌺🪻💐🌼🌻\r\n💖🌸💗🌷💕🌹💞🌺💓🪻💘💐💝🌼💖🌻\r\n🌷🌸🌹 **for my sweet thaii, with all the love and affection from your batsy** 🌹🌸🌷\r\n\r\n#Nocticula #Lady Briarwood #HarleyQuinn #She'sAwesome #She'sSweet #ThankYouMyDearFriend #ForSharingYourGamesWithMe #ILoveYouuuu 🔥🎮❤️",
